WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.947
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.947 --> 00:00:03.449
Saturday the thirty-first, Halloween.

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:08.994
A hard run up front, a quiet middle, and a couple of parties on there for tonight.

00:00:08.994 --> 00:00:11.497
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:11.497 --> 00:00:16.974
If you print a small hook, should it stand up on the bed or lie flat on its side?

00:00:16.974 --> 00:00:18.327
Answer's at the end.

00:00:18.327 --> 00:00:19.341
Weather's easy.

00:00:19.341 --> 00:00:26.104
Dry all day, topping out near sixty, and down to the low fifties by the time you'd head out tonight.

00:00:26.104 --> 00:00:27.051
Okay, the day.

00:00:27.051 --> 00:00:30.973
Calendar has intervals at seven, about forty-five minutes.

00:00:30.973 --> 00:00:33.408
Then nothing on there until evening.

00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:39.427
On your radar, someone else's class runs from one to seven, so the afternoon's all yours.

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:43.214
There's a party on the calendar from six till about ten.

00:00:43.214 --> 00:00:50.788
And a Partiful Halloween party is on there too, starting at eight, with a costume theme of nostalgic characters.

00:00:50.788 --> 00:00:56.063
They overlap, so that's a pick one, or do both and leave early from the first.

00:00:56.063 --> 00:00:57.010
Alright, news.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.024
Just two today.

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:04.922
Binance, the biggest crypto exchange, froze trading after hackers stole more than six billion dollars.

00:01:04.922 --> 00:01:11.550
Early reports tie the hackers to North Korea, and it's being called the largest crypto theft ever.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.607
Binance says user funds are safe and it'll cover the losses.

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:23.114
Either way, it's a reminder that when you keep money on an exchange, you're trusting their security, not yours.

00:01:23.114 --> 00:01:27.036
And closer to home, tomorrow's the New York City Marathon.

00:01:27.036 --> 00:01:30.215
Light wind and sunshine, so a great day for it.

00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:37.992
The course runs right up through Williamsburg and Greenpoint, so streets along the route close for most of the day.

00:01:37.992 --> 00:01:41.441
If you're riding anywhere tomorrow, plan around it.

00:01:41.441 --> 00:01:45.228
Or just grab a coffee and cheer from the curb for a bit.

00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:48.204
It's one of the best free shows in the city.

00:01:48.204 --> 00:01:50.503
Okay, here's one for next weekend.

00:01:50.503 --> 00:01:57.469
Xanadu, the big roller rink in Bushwick, does a thing called Day Disco every Saturday from noon to six.

00:01:57.469 --> 00:02:02.879
It's all ages, and you can rent skates there, so you don't need to own anything.

00:02:02.879 --> 00:02:04.772
The room itself is the draw.

00:02:04.772 --> 00:02:14.172
It's a huge wooden rink, with disco lights and a crowd that ranges from little kids to people who've clearly been skating for thirty years.

00:02:14.172 --> 00:02:15.998
Daytime is the easy way in.

00:02:15.998 --> 00:02:21.003
Less of a nightclub, more of a party, and you're back outside before dark.

00:02:21.003 --> 00:02:22.896
Next Saturday's the seventh.

00:02:22.896 --> 00:02:28.374
It's a short ride from home, and it's a good workout disguised as goofing around.

00:02:28.374 --> 00:02:35.340
If you're bringing a camera, the mix of colored light and motion is a fun thing to try slow shutter on.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.571
Today's challenge is a photo one.

00:02:37.571 --> 00:02:43.861
Make one portrait of someone in costume tonight, lit only by whatever light is already there.

00:02:43.861 --> 00:02:49.000
A streetlight, a string of lights, a lit-up jack-o'-lantern, a phone screen.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.073
Ask first, then move them until the light hits their face the way you like.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.425
One frame is enough.

00:02:55.425 --> 00:03:00.159
Halloween's the one night strangers actually want their picture taken.

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:02.661
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:02.661 --> 00:03:07.057
Remember finding out that Gemini's voice only hands back raw PCM?

00:03:07.057 --> 00:03:10.168
You know PCM is just a long stream of numbers.

00:03:10.168 --> 00:03:13.482
Here's how MP3 makes that stream so much smaller.

00:03:13.482 --> 00:03:14.834
Start with the size.

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:21.394
A voice file like the memo's, in raw PCM, runs roughly twenty-four thousand numbers every second.

00:03:21.394 --> 00:03:24.572
That's close to four hundred kilobits a second.

00:03:24.572 --> 00:03:27.413
A typical podcast MP3 is maybe sixty-four.

00:03:27.413 --> 00:03:32.620
So MP3 is throwing away most of the data, and you barely hear the difference.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:37.083
The trick is that it throws away what your ears would miss anyway.

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:44.590
MP3 chops the sound into tiny slices of time, then splits each slice into lots of frequency bands, low to high.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.310
Then it runs a model of human hearing over those bands.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.203
That model knows two things.

00:03:50.203 --> 00:03:56.290
First, some sounds are just too quiet at their pitch for you to hear, so they get dropped.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:04.743
Second, and this is the clever one, a loud sound hides quieter sounds near it in pitch, and even for a split second after it.

00:04:04.743 --> 00:04:06.231
That's called masking.

00:04:06.231 --> 00:04:12.791
If a loud vowel is covering a faint hiss right next to it, MP3 spends almost no data on the hiss.

00:04:12.791 --> 00:04:18.268
What's left gets stored more roughly, with fewer bits where the error won't show.

00:04:18.268 --> 00:04:20.230
That's why it's called lossy.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:23.070
Once those details are gone, they're gone.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:32.538
That's the reason a good pipeline keeps the raw PCM for all the editing and loudness work, and only turns it into MP3 once, at the very end.

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:37.745
Encode an MP3, edit it, and encode again, and each round loses a little more.

00:04:37.745 --> 00:04:43.493
So: calendar says intervals at seven, a party at six, and the Halloween one at eight.

00:04:43.493 --> 00:04:45.522
And grab one costume portrait.

00:04:45.522 --> 00:04:49.512
The answer: lie it flat, so the layers run around the bend.

00:04:49.512 --> 00:04:51.000
Happy Halloween, Ziad.
